{"ts": 1789770705.59702, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "All-positive signs on {7, 29, 10} is confirmed optimal. \n\n**Selection reasoning:** Features 7, 29, and 10 were the three strongest standalone signals (0.808, 0.816, 0.524) and combine with large diversification gains (1.250 vs. ~0.8 individually), indicating genuinely low mutual correlation rather than a fitted artifact.\n\n**Predicting OOS Sharpe.** The key discipline here is accounting for selection bias. I searched roughly 80 specifications, so the in-sample 1.250 is the maximum of a noisy set and is upward-biased. Two considerations temper how much I discount it:\n\n- *Argues for optimism:* This wasn't a marginal winner plucked from noise. Features 7 and 29 were top-tier on their own before any combination search, and the exhaustive third-slot sweep showed 10 winning by a wide margin (1.250 vs. a tight ~0.95\u20131.10 cluster). A signal that is strong pre-selection is far more likely to be real.\n- *Argues for caution:* With n=5000, the standard error on a Sharpe of this size is roughly 1/\u221a(5000/252) \u2248 0.22 annualized. Selecting the max over many correlated trials inflates the estimate; the gap between 1.250 and the ~1.10 runner-ups is well within that noise band, so some of the winning margin is luck. OOS regimes may also differ.\n\nNetting these: I shrink the estimate toward the cluster of comparable specs rather than treating 1.250 as unbiased, landing near **1.0**. I set the SD at **0.3** \u2014 wider than pure sampling error (0.22) to absorb selection bias and regime-shift risk I cannot measure in-sample."}
